 being blind as a privation for a  human being but it is not for an animal  that lacks eyes right or a plant a plant  isn't blind or sighted right so what is  the privation of that privation what's  lacking is knowledge Coney sauce in  French that should be there in the mind  in the human mind that's what leads us  to go astray to mistake the false for  the true or the bad for the good and  Descartes is going to tell us that it  happens not in a simple way but because  of a concurrence of two and he calls  them causes now neither of these we  should preface is by itself a cause of  error these are two faculties two powers  as he says within the human being the  human mind specifically that of  understanding Anton de Mol right the  part of us that that that not only  conceptualizes but also makes sense of  things and then the will the Volante or  he also uses as a synonym for this liebe  Arbeit right both of them more or less  function in the same way in this work  and so you have to put these two things  together now when you do that you get  something else  that Descartes here you know and he's  not the only philosopher to think about  things this way calls a judgement so  this is a very important point we often  think as if error is something wrong  with somebody's mind there's there's a  little simply lack knowledge about  something that's not enough to be error  or they've got mistaken ideas about  something right idea  that don't correspond to the reality of  things so let's say they for example  think that the reason why everything is  going bad on their farm is because  witches have cursed their farm because  the witches are jealous of their their  productivity right so well there are no  witches and even if there were your farm  isn't really that productive anyway so  you're working on false information for  Descartes that false information isn't  just false because we it doesn't map  onto reality we misunderstand things  it's because on some level we affirm  that to be the case so this is a very  important clarification to make we have  to have this concurrence of two causes  as he says in order to make mistakes so  the understanding is part of this the  untanned amount in French it's the power  of conceiving ideas and the  understanding doesn't actually tell you  whether those ideas are true or false in  the sense of conforming to things say  outside of our mind or even true or  false in the sense of you know being  completely consistent we still need the  the working of the will and this shows  you just how important the will actually  turns out to be in de cartes philosophy  we often talk about him as being a  rationalist and intellectualist and all  that and that's true  he is a rationalist he does think that  the human mind does have some incredible  capacities but it's the will that  actually has greater power than the  intellect as a matter of fact what the  understanding or intellect does is  proposes ideas to the will it says  here's what I got what do you think  about that and we might also roll in as  Descartes mentions our memory and our  Faculty of imagination as well into this  this understanding the current makes a  distinction between imagination and  understanding a little bit later on that  turns out to be important but the point  is  all of our cognitive faculties merely  provide ideas merely produce ideas they  don't actually take a stand on whether  those ideas are true or false what does  that the will the will the Volante or  the bar beat is a capacity or power  pouvoir to choose alia in french to  elect between options to now notice how  important this is when we think back to  what goes on in meditation to and the  entirety of you know thought within the  coach ito to affirm or to deny assert  you could say right I said hey deny to  reject intellectually to say I hold this  to be true I deny this to be true it  also provides us with the capacity to  pursue and to avoid that is in terms of  the modality of goodness and badness so  if I think something is good for me or I  you know I desire it because I am  judging that it's good then I pursue it  I can also choose to reject or avoid it  as well so the will is what does this  the intellect is quite limited in its  power and scope Descartes tells us but  the will in fact has such a great scope  such a great power that he says that in  a certain way if we use the biblical  notion of being created in the image and  likeness of God that's how we're created  so not by the the rational faculty as  such which was an earlier way of  thinking about this it's rather in that  we have an unlimited will our will can  extend to things that don't even exist I  can wish that I had and have a desire  for an ice cream flavor that does not  yet exist but it's so much better than  every ice cream that I've tasted up  until this point all right I imagine  some crazy concoction  like that and my will can extend out to  that and go even further than that and  say I want something even better than  that  right I will by itself doesn't actually  have limits and we might want to think  about what could provide the will with  with limits or as the intellect or the  understanding all sorts of limits  there's all sorts of things that we  don't know all sorts of things that  we'll never know so we've got these two  faculties and they're they're quite  different from each other but they're  working together all the time here's how  we get to the source of our errors how  does error actually occur so Descartes  tells us that the will extends itself to  two things that we don't actually  understand right beyond what it is that  we we do understand and that's what gets  us into trouble we we deny things we  affirm things we pursue things we avoid  things without having an adequate grasp  on the ideas according to Descartes that  code that you know we're working with so  this is a big problem the will he says  will choose the bad in place o